# machines/<hostname>.conf.sample - the machine file teeup sources after the
# answers file (lib/answers.sh: answers_load), so anything set here wins over
# an answer, and it is the ONLY place a work identity is configured: the
# wizard never asks about work (see .superpowers/identity/brief.md,
# 2026-09-17).
#
# The normal place for it is your own config directory, never this checkout:
# copy this sample to $TEEUP_CONFIG_DIR/machines/$(hostname -s).conf (usually
# ~/.config/teeup/machines/), edit it, and it survives every `git pull`
# because it was never in the repo to begin with. machines/<hostname>.conf
# here in the checkout still works -- teeup checks it second, when your own
# file is absent -- and is what a fork of teeup uses instead.
#
# Every key below is optional; a machine with no work identity needs no file
# at all.

# A work identity exists only when this is set. It gives the ssh capability a
# second ed25519 key (~/.ssh/id_ed25519_work), the git capability still signs
# with the personal key alone (git has one identity, full stop), and the
# github capability uploads the work key to whatever host TEEUP_WORK_GH_HOST
# names below.
#TEEUP_WORK_EMAIL="ada@corp.example"

# The GitHub host the work key uploads to. Defaults to github.com when
# TEEUP_WORK_EMAIL is set and this is not. Name a GitHub Enterprise host here
# when work lives on one.
#TEEUP_WORK_GH_HOST="github.enterprise.example.com"

# The GitHub login the work key belongs to, when work is a second account on
# the same host as personal (usually github.com). `gh` targets a host, and a
# host has exactly one active account, so this is the only thing that tells
# two github.com accounts apart: teeup runs
# `gh auth switch --hostname <host> --user <account>` before the work upload
# and switches back afterwards. Both accounts must already be signed in
# (`gh auth login`). Without this, and with work on the same host as personal,
# teeup refuses the work upload rather than putting the work key on the
# personal account. A work identity on its own host does not need it.
#TEEUP_WORK_GH_ACCOUNT="ada-at-work"

# Hard constraints unrelated to identity, shown here because they are the
# other common reason a machine file exists at all.
#TEEUP_PACKAGE_MANAGER="macports"
#TEEUP_SKIP="aerospace"
#TEEUP_THEME="catppuccin"
